{"resourceIdentifier":"4996","resourceName":"AVOIDANCE OF METHANE EMISSION THROUGH VERMICOMPOST GENERATION IN THE TEA ESTATES OF AMALGAMATED PLANTATIONS PVT LTD (APPL), ASSAM AND NORTH BENGAL, INDIA","description":"The current project activity is implemented by Amalgamated Plantations Private limited (APPL) in its 25 tea estates, located in the diverse agro-ecological regions of two different states, Assam and West Bengal. Common practices in the project area are tea cultivation which include conventional methods such as synthetic fertilizer application, tillage, insecticide application, pesticide application and high-irrigation water consumption. These conventional practices contribute to soil depletion and accelerate GHG emissions. To address these challenges, the project proponent, APPL has ventured into sustainable green initiatives by the production of vermicompost. The construction of vermicompost units and subsequent generation of vermicompost, avoids the production of methane from the agricultural residues and cow dung manure; which would otherwise have been left to decay anaerobically within the project site, and cow dung pits/anaerobic lagoons. The vermicompost produced is then applied to the tea bushes to improve the soil structure and nutrient pool. This is further reflected by quantifying the reduction in emission of greenhouse gases through the project activity, i.e., the production of vermicompost from the wastes. In addition, the vermicompost generated is a powerful soil property enhancer as it is able to transform decomposable material into nutrient-rich organic manure. Hence the project will provide replicable model for environment friendly and economically viable for vermicompost production. During the 10-year crediting period, it is estimated that there will be an average annual reduction and removal of 2,51,964 tCO2e from 2,49,854.39 Metric Tonnes of waste","location":{"latitude":27.353792,"longitude":95.648636},"inPublicCommentPeriod":false,"la